ctly - doors, trapdoors, curtains, furniture; the 3-D objects changed to portray vertically too; the printed symbols from simple to elaborate); Splitting the Grid (the altitude mimicry by cut and shifted pieces; the tracing method - the paper pulled down to the scaled height; the photocopy method - the cut-outs placed at the proper elevation, the 20-foot platform twice the 10-foot-square distance above the cut line); Horizon Lines (the horizontal orientation; the perpendicular verticals with the T-square or the 90-degree triangle); Geomorphic Mapping (the time problem and the unpredictable PCs; the same-shape sections with the standardized edge points abutting any neighbor - the tremendous combinations; the underground, city, and fortress suitability; the small-part sections with their several unused areas; the shapes - the easiest squares, the three-orientation triangles needing more, the many-position harder hexagons; the planned connections; the standardized symmetric locations - the exact center or the 1/3-in pairs; the 60 x 60-foot section with its one or two connections per side against the square-mile realm's six to eight; the 75% entry rule against the enclosing dead ends); and Using Geomorphs (the random generation of huge areas with the deliberate exceptions; the numbered geomorphs and the d3/d4/d6 placement rolls for which face is north; the placed section as normal setting - the matching edge connections proceeding unnoticed, the mismatches dead-ending; the variety and orientations hiding the revisited sections) - the round ending before the perspective grid plates, which are graphics and pin nothing.",
